- **Cost.** In the library's measurements, writing 200 customer objects takes 134 µs with `Preserve` against 50 µs with no handler. Each object costs a dictionary lookup, an identifier string, and a namespaced attribute.
- **What gets an identifier.** Objects with members do. Strings, numbers, other simple values, and collections do not.
- **Collection items.** A repeated object is replaced by a reference when a member holds it. An object that appears twice as an item of a collection is written in full both times.
- **`IgnoreCycles` is broader than its name.** It writes nil for every repeated object, including one that two members share without forming a cycle. System.Text.Json writes `null` only for a true cycle.
- **Custom handlers.** A class derived from `XmlReferenceHandler` supplies the resolver that is used when reading. When writing, `id` and `ref` attributes are written only for the built-in `Preserve` instance. With any other handler, a repeated object is written as nil.
- **Sequences.** `SerializeAsyncEnumerable` and `DeserializeAsyncEnumerable` don't apply the handler.
- **Source generation.** A source-generated context in the default mode or in `Metadata` mode applies the handler as the reflection resolver does.
- **Thread safety.** `Preserve` and `IgnoreCycles` are stateless singletons. Each call to `Serialize` or `Deserialize` gets its own resolver, so options that use them can be shared between threads.
